1. What this is

A deliberately vulnerable clinic appointment web application, running inside an isolated Ubuntu VM, that I can attack and investigate without any risk to my host machine or any other network. This is the foundation lab for the rest of the internship (log analysis, hardening, and final hand-over).

2. Lab architecture

| Component | Detail |
|---|---|
| Virtualization | VirtualBox VM (Ubuntu) |
| VM name | `Ubuntu` (network labelled `CLINIC-LAB`) |
| Network mode | Host-only / internal network adapter — no NAT, no default gateway |
| Static IP | `192.168.50.10/24` on `enp0s3` |
| Netplan config | `ipv4.never-default: true`, IPv6 disabled |
| Application | `clinic_app.py` — Python `http.server` app, port `8000` |
| Database | `clinic.db` — SQLite, seeded with sample appointment records |

3. Proof of network isolation

The VM has a static IP on an internal-only network and no route to the outside world:

```
$ ping -c 2 8.8.8.8
ping: connect: Network is unreachable

$ ping -c 2 192.168.50.10
64 bytes from 192.168.50.10: icmp_seq=1 ttl=64 time=0.040 ms
64 bytes from 192.168.50.10: icmp_seq=2 ttl=64 time=0.402 ms
```

The netplan configuration confirms `ipv4.never-default: true` (no default gateway is ever installed) and IPv6 is disabled, so the VM has no path off the internal `192.168.50.10/24` segment:

- `docs/isolation.png` — ping to `8.8.8.8` fails ("Network is unreachable"); ping to the lab IP succeeds
- `docs/lab-network.png` — `ip addr` and `netplan` config showing the static internal IP and no default route

